diff --git a/docs/V169_RUNTIME_DATABASE_GUARD_MONITOR.md b/docs/V169_RUNTIME_DATABASE_GUARD_MONITOR.md
new file mode 100644
index 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000..f4837cfe1718bad2409fb398825c612616db95ee
--- /dev/null
+++ b/docs/V169_RUNTIME_DATABASE_GUARD_MONITOR.md
@@ -0,0 +1,65 @@
+# V169 Runtime Database Guard Monitor
+
+Release: `17.0.0-selfbuilt.169-runtime-guard-monitor`
+
+## Change
+
+Every Render portal instance now starts a mandatory runtime PostgreSQL guard
+monitor after Nitro starts and outside the advisory-lock scheduler leader. The
+startup transaction remains the first gate. The runtime monitor then reuses the
+same frozen `assertCriticalDatabaseGuards` contract to verify all nine protected
+triggers and the five unique guard functions every 60 seconds by default.
+
+Each cycle creates a new PostgreSQL client with a fixed application name. The
+connection and server statement timeouts default to 10 seconds, the client query
+timeout is 11 seconds, lock waits are limited to two seconds, and client cleanup
+is limited to two seconds. The next unreferenced timer is scheduled only after
+the preceding connection, attestation and cleanup complete, so checks cannot
+overlap or accumulate.
+
+The interval can be set between 15 and 300 seconds with
+`PORTAL_DATABASE_GUARD_MONITOR_INTERVAL_MS`. The server timeout can be set
+between one and 30 seconds with
+`PORTAL_DATABASE_GUARD_MONITOR_QUERY_TIMEOUT_MS`. Invalid values fall back to
+the safe defaults. There is no monitor disable switch.
+
+## Failure behavior
+
+A client-construction, connection, metadata-query, attestation or cleanup
+failure stops the monitor and exits the instance exactly once. The log contains
+only a fixed phase and a constrained database error code; it never includes the
+connection string, query, guard source or provider data. Render can restart the
+instance, but the existing startup attestation continues to reject drift before
+the service becomes ready. Runtime drift is detected, never repaired.
+
+Successful verification is logged once when the monitor becomes ready. Later
+successful cycles remain silent. The monitor uses an unreferenced idle timer and
+does not replace Node's default SIGTERM or SIGINT behavior.
+
+## Mobile navigation correction
+
+The same release corrects the mobile member dock so Home and Games are separate
+current destinations inside the lobby. Games, provider and category actions
+retain Games as the only active `aria-current` item; Home restores Home, and the
+other dock destinations keep their own state. The correction changes navigation
+state only and does not mark any game or provider playable.
+
+## Verification and scope
+
+Synthetic tests cover immediate verification, post-completion scheduling,
+non-overlap, idempotent stop, stop during an in-flight query, bounded cleanup,
+sanitized connection/query/cleanup failures, exact-once exit, and per-instance
+wiring outside scheduler leadership. The isolated PostgreSQL 18 suite replaces
+an expected function body under the same name after a successful runtime check,
+proves the next monitor cycle exits fail closed, proves the drift is not
+repaired, and then restores the function explicitly.
+
+Mobile navigation tests also verify a single active destination, distinct Home
+and Games state, correct Wallet/Promotions/Support state, and Games retention
+through provider and category entry points.
+
+- no migration, table, column, backup format or backup data-contract change;
+- backup schema remains `0122-acceptance-ledger-integrity` with 76 tables;
+- no payment, payout, wallet, game-provider, DNS, credential, plan or
+  production-control change;
+- no live provider or customer transaction calls during validation.
